Measured 2026-08-28 while checking #79's model against the captured
fixtures, and it deserves a test of its own because it is the passthrough
invariant proving itself against reality rather than against a test the
same author wrote.

Every fixture in t/fixtures/ was fed through its generated class:

  ContainerInspectResponse   1 object    no unknown fields
  ContainerSummary           2 objects   no unknown fields
  Network / Volume           2 each      no unknown fields
  SystemInfo / SystemVersion 1 each      no unknown fields
  ImageSummary               2 objects   unknown: VirtualSize

VirtualSize is in spec/v1.41.yaml and gone from spec/v1.44.yaml onward --
Docker removed it from the swagger, and the engine that produced the
fixture still sends it. The model forwarded it verbatim instead of
dropping it, which is exactly invariant 2 of the type model.

The test: run every t/fixtures/*.json through the class its endpoint
returns and assert the round trip loses nothing -- TO_JSON of the
inflated object must contain every key the fixture had. It fails the day
someone makes the model tidy by discarding what it does not recognise,
which is the change that would quietly cost this distribution the
property that a newer (or older) engine works on day one.

Note the fixtures are the only real daemon output in the default suite;
no live access needed.

Done in 327bcd7 as t/type_fixture_passthrough.t: every t/fixtures/*.json fed through the generated class its endpoint returns, asserting no fixture key goes missing from TO_JSON, with a dedicated subtest pinning ImageSummary's VirtualSize -- in spec/v1.41.yaml, gone from v1.44 onward, still served by the engine. The test-writer reproduced the defect (deleting the key before TO_JSON) before trusting it. Fixture-only. It now runs through from_data, following k85's split of the two entry points.
